00:14:14,470 --> 00:14:23,350
and characteristics that you could address and the need to narrow down what the interviewer is looking for.

222
00:14:23,350 --> 00:14:27,590
So if you ask them question, which part of Uber do you want me to design? What are you going to get back?

223
00:14:27,600 --> 00:14:31,590
That's a great question. And here's where luck plays a role.

224
00:14:31,590 --> 00:14:33,350
But I would say it's good to be prepared for it.

225
00:14:33,350 --> 00:14:37,110
If you were to ask me or if I was to ask you as interviewer,

226
00:14:37,110 --> 00:14:38,949
hey Robert, what should I design?

227
00:14:38,949 --> 00:14:43,110
You could be generous and tell me well design the driver matching feature for me.

228
00:14:43,110 --> 00:14:47,670
If you were generous, you might even tell me you have a system where you open the phone, you're on the phone, you're going to see a map.

229
00:14:47,670 --> 00:14:50,790
The map is going to show all these little dots.

230
00:14:50,790 --> 00:14:52,629
Each dot represents drivers in your vicinity or some range.

231
00:14:52,629 --> 00:14:54,629
I want to track that.

232
00:14:54,629 --> 00:14:59,030
On the other extreme, you might have an interviewer who might say, "Look, we are Uber.

233
00:14:59,030 --> 00:15:04,151
We are in year one of launch and we only have so many users. Our goal is to build engagement.

234
00:15:04,160 --> 00:15:07,189
What kind of features do you think we might need?

235
00:15:07,189 --> 00:15:08,790
It sounds like a very product management question.

236
00:15:08,790 --> 00:15:11,110
And it kind of is.

237
00:15:11,110 --> 00:15:12,871
But you will, if you get this, you get it. You got to roll with it.

238
00:15:12,871 --> 00:15:14,710
So this is why it's a good thing.

239
00:15:14,710 --> 00:15:18,230
that you only have 25 or so systems to kind of prepare for.

240
00:15:18,230 --> 00:15:20,550
That gives you time to understand the market,

241
00:15:20,550 --> 00:15:22,870
the strategy, what that system is about.

242
00:15:22,870 --> 00:15:26,230
and get a better grasp of what you might want to build.

243
00:15:26,230 --> 00:15:27,910
So you can actually offer options on what you want to build.

244
00:15:27,910 --> 00:15:29,430
It shows two things.

245
00:15:29,430 --> 00:15:30,949
One, it shows your seniority that you worked in a system.

246
00:15:30,949 --> 00:15:33,509
that went from 1 to million to 10 million users.

247
00:15:33,519 --> 00:15:39,189
It also shows that look at some point in time you're going to go.

248
00:15:39,189 --> 00:15:41,030
well I spoke about driver matching.

249
00:15:41,030 --> 00:15:43,110
and now I know that when I have to book an order.

250
00:15:43,110 --> 00:15:50,151
I have to make sure that in a heavily congested area these drivers don't end up getting double. booked.

251
00:15:50,151 --> 00:15:56,150
So able to form those connections across different departments in a product.

252
00:15:56,150 --> 00:16:00,389
and as a principal engineer you will see that you might not see that as a new engineer.

253
00:16:00,389 --> 00:16:04,710
So if you ever ask a question go prepared but sometimes there's only so much you can do.

254
00:16:04,720 --> 00:16:09,270
Sometimes you don't know what you're going to get. Change tracks here.

255
00:16:09,270 --> 00:16:14,150
There is a question that you proposed sounds very interesting to me.

256
00:16:14,150 --> 00:16:15,910
and I don't know what I'm going to get.

257
00:16:15,910 --> 00:16:22,870
What is the history of this system design interview track. and how has it evolved over the past decade?

258
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:26,389
I think the evolution has been over the last two decades.

259
00:16:26,389 --> 00:16:30,230
and this is only my observation and I don't claim to know the full history.

260
00:16:30,230 --> 00:16:33,590
but what I've observed is I think early 2000s just before.

261
00:16:33,590 --> 00:16:36,470
or around the kind of bubble and burst.

262
00:16:36,470 --> 00:16:43,670
a lot of the design work or some design work was largely enterprisedriven.

263
00:16:43,670 --> 00:16:48,949
I started my career in telecommunications and the closest to design.

264
00:16:48,949 --> 00:16:54,310
that I had done at that time 25 years ago was building data centers.

265
00:16:54,310 --> 00:16:55,829
Now before you think that's cool, data centers back.

266
00:16:55,829 --> 00:17:01,030
then were literally one or two racks at a time in a giant building.

267
00:17:01,030 --> 00:17:04,390
That was a data center.

268
00:17:04,390 --> 00:17:09,750
And a lot of the expertise needed back then was your Cisco certified XYZ,

269
00:17:09,750 --> 00:17:13,110
your Microsoft certified ABC and your vendor certified so and so.

270
00:17:13,120 --> 00:17:18,870
And you'll find that a lot of these interviews were focused around certifications.

271
00:17:18,870 --> 00:17:20,789
and enterprise specific or vendor specific things.

272
00:17:20,789 --> 00:17:24,470
Now as companies like Google and Amazon and the early era companies took off,

273
00:17:24,470 --> 00:17:27,110
they started building a lot of things in house.

274
00:17:27,110 --> 00:17:30,950
In fact, when I spoke to somebody 20 20 years ago at Cisco, they claimed that Google was their biggest threat.

275
00:17:30,960 --> 00:17:36,630
And I was surprised because Google was a search company and Cisco was a networking giant.

276
00:17:36,630 --> 00:17:39,110
How can they be in the same realm?

277
00:17:39,110 --> 00:17:41,110
But unbeknownst to me at the time,

278
00:17:41,110 --> 00:17:47,270
Google was building their own data centers, their own custom networking switches, their own topologies, protocols, whatnot.

279
00:17:47,270 --> 00:17:52,070
So I mean as these unicorns or these giants were becoming giants they would start seeing.

280
00:17:52,070 --> 00:17:56,230
that folks who came with pure vendor skills.

281
00:17:56,240 --> 00:18:02,391
would not easily switch to like would not switch their skills to different domain. or different way of doing.

282
00:18:02,391 --> 00:18:07,029
So they started going more and more fundamental in how they would interview candidates.

283
00:18:07,029 --> 00:18:10,950
Initially Google even had those counting number of balls in a bus kind of questions.

284
00:18:10,950 --> 00:18:12,789
which has long been outlawed.

285
00:18:12,789 --> 00:18:14,789
But what it gave way to was really thinking about systems,

286
00:18:14,789 --> 00:18:20,630
thinking about quantitative methods, thinking about reasoning kind of like causal reasoning.

287
00:18:20,630 --> 00:18:23,110
If the load goes up here, what happens over there?

288
00:18:23,110 --> 00:18:25,909
And from that they found that regardless of your background,

289
00:18:25,909 --> 00:18:30,870
if you can think in a systematic way and think of these basic constructs.

290
00:18:30,870 --> 00:18:33,110
like you know Q's and Q mean I mean Q's in a system,

291
00:18:33,110 --> 00:18:36,870
things like arrival rates, things like service rates,

292
00:18:36,870 --> 00:18:39,990
things like if my node can process so many requests, what happens over there?

293
00:18:39,990 --> 00:18:46,470
They found that these candidates would get better at thinking at incrementally higher level of granularity.

294
00:18:46,470 --> 00:18:51,350
So design rounds became more and more about the concepts of building ambiguous systems.

295
00:18:51,350 --> 00:18:53,669
that may not even be real.

296
00:18:53,669 --> 00:18:56,710
but could stress your thinking on a very vendor agnostic way.

297
00:18:56,720 --> 00:19:00,470
So I can talk about when I failed my first design interview at at Google 2008.

298
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:04,710
and this is when I saw one of these questions for the first time.

299
00:19:04,710 --> 00:19:08,549
and last 10 15 years this has been a staple for student design interviews.

300
00:19:08,549 --> 00:19:12,789
You might see the depth and scope and expectations gotten higher in the last few years,

301
00:19:12,789 --> 00:19:18,311
but this structure has been fairly consistent. I would say 2010 onwards.

302
00:19:18,320 --> 00:19:22,950
You said just now when you failed your first system design interview at Google.

303
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:24,789
I'd love to hear that story.

304
00:19:24,799 --> 00:19:27,868
There's nothing quite like exposing yourself on live radio, is there?

305
00:19:27,878 --> 00:19:30,470
[laughter]

306
00:19:30,470 --> 00:19:34,871
Well, actually, so my first design interview that I failed was in 2008. Sadly, he wasn't my last.

307
00:19:34,871 --> 00:19:38,950
I would continue to fail them for another I think under five or so years.

308
00:19:38,950 --> 00:19:40,789
Sadly, without even knowing why I was failing.

309
00:19:40,799 --> 00:19:45,751
The way I would fail them and later I would see that was a pretty common thing. I mean I wasn't unique there was.

310
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:51,750
that I would go into a Sims interview and somebody would ask me you know design X.

311
00:19:51,750 --> 00:19:56,390
and I would suddenly throw things like caches and components and technologies on the board.

312
00:19:56,390 --> 00:19:59,270
without understanding what the problem was about, what are the requirements,

313
00:19:59,270 --> 00:20:03,510
what are the behavioral expectations or the performance expectations of a system.

314
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:07,430
Actually, the first one I was ever asked was,

315
00:20:07,430 --> 00:20:09,430
you had to push 100 terabytes of data into a data center.

316
00:20:09,430 --> 00:20:11,830
How would you do this?

317
00:20:11,830 --> 00:20:15,669
Looking back now, you break it down and you can do it in a series of steps.

318
00:20:15,669 --> 00:20:17,909
But I just came in with, well, I would use FTP.

319
00:20:17,909 --> 00:20:20,470
That was my vague response, right?

320
00:20:20,470 --> 00:20:23,590
And that opened up a whole rabbit hole of, well, what about this, what about that, what about that?

321
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:26,630
It became a pretty much a catamask game where I was playing catchup.

322
00:20:26,630 --> 00:20:30,070
And I would do this for many years without knowing why I would fail the interviews, right?

323
00:20:30,080 --> 00:20:34,790
I mean, I would come out of this thinking that, hey, I had all the right buzzwords. I had FTP in there.

324
00:20:34,790 --> 00:20:36,311
I had pipes in there. I had switches in there.

325
00:20:36,320 --> 00:20:37,909
I had everything in there.

326
00:20:37,909 --> 00:20:41,750
But I still failed.

327
00:20:41,750 --> 00:20:43,990
Around 2016, I was at an Uber interview which forever changed my life.

328
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:49,190
The question was designing Twitter and same thing.

329
00:20:49,190 --> 00:20:54,950
I was throwing around things like cues and load balancers and nodes and Dynamo DBs and whatnot.

330
00:20:54,950 --> 00:21:01,029
I was struggling and the interviewer I think had a pained look on his face.

331
00:21:01,029 --> 00:21:03,110
So in a very rare act of generosity,

332
00:21:03,110 --> 00:21:04,630
he kind of asked me one question.

333
00:21:04,630 --> 00:21:08,789
and the question was what would be the first request that would fail.

334
00:21:08,789 --> 00:21:12,950
and that was towards the end of the interview and I packed up and I went home.

335
00:21:12,950 --> 00:21:16,230
Weirdly enough when I went home I ran across this book here.

336
00:21:16,230 --> 00:21:24,071
I'm pointing to a book called database management systems by Ragu Ramakrishna and Gerke. It's a book on databases.

337
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:27,510
More specifically, it's a book on database internals.

338
00:21:27,510 --> 00:21:32,230
And what it talks about is how do indexes work in a database? What kind of latencies they give you?

339
00:21:32,230 --> 00:21:34,710
What kind of response times they give you?

340
00:21:34,710 --> 00:21:36,710
So that question made me look at this book.

341
00:21:36,710 --> 00:21:41,909
and realize well what if I had asked how many requests will this system be getting?

342
00:21:41,909 --> 00:21:44,710
If you got to dump it all into database, when would the first failure be?

343
00:21:44,710 --> 00:21:48,471
The first failure would be when the database cannot handle load. When can it not handle a load?

344
00:21:48,471 --> 00:21:50,310
And then work backwards.

345
00:21:50,310 --> 00:21:51,909
Suddenly, I don't know if you played that game,

346
00:21:51,909 --> 00:21:56,230
uh, the incredible machine back in the '9s, you would build these Rube Goldberg machines.

347
00:21:56,240 --> 00:22:01,669
where you light a candle, the candle blows a balloon, the balloon flows up and turns switch on.

348
00:22:01,669 --> 00:22:06,870
and the switch turns on a fan which hits a cat, some weird machine, right?

349
00:22:06,870 --> 00:22:09,029
And you kind of think of that.

350
00:22:09,029 --> 00:22:14,549
and you work backwards and each of these components becomes part of that larger system. So, this interview kind of made me realize, okay,

351
00:22:14,549 --> 00:22:17,590
if I can break my problem down into these steps and I'll talk about those steps too, right?

352
00:22:17,600 --> 00:22:21,110
like ask for the requirements look at exactly what are the SLOs's.

353
00:22:21,110 --> 00:22:26,789
if I need to put something in Twitter as a user how much delay am I allowed to.

354
00:22:26,789 --> 00:22:30,950
tolerate as a feed consumer.

355
00:22:30,950 --> 00:22:34,790
am I okay if I see a post from you taking more than 10 seconds more than 5. seconds more than 20 seconds.

356
00:22:34,799 --> 00:22:36,630
like where would I be happy about.

357
00:22:36,630 --> 00:22:39,750
so putting these numbers in place like kind of helped me learn about.

358
00:22:39,750 --> 00:22:43,350
okay what if I design a system that matches these different criterias.

359
00:22:43,350 --> 00:22:47,590
so that was kind of the aha moment I guess on how I started learning about it.

360
00:22:47,590 --> 00:22:49,750
I would still fail But at least now I know why I was failing.

361
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:53,029
Yeah.

362
00:22:53,029 --> 00:22:57,430
So you've talked about failures learning from failure and how you've evolved your approach.

363
00:22:57,430 --> 00:23:02,870
Is there any key takeaways or lessons learned from that process that you haven't covered?

364
00:23:02,880 --> 00:23:09,830
I know that Uber failure and recovery seems like a rocky style montage over the weekend,

365
00:23:09,830 --> 00:23:11,750
but reality was that aha moment was pure luck.

366
00:23:11,750 --> 00:23:13,750
I happened to have that book at at home and I found it.

367
00:23:13,750 --> 00:23:17,990
But what I did thankfully do was reach out to a few of my friends.

368
00:23:17,990 --> 00:23:21,270
and pitch to them my thoughts of why had gone wrong. At that time my network was pretty small.

369
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:25,750
So I had to kind of figure out on my own. So the friends who had I mean I mean my colleagues.

370
00:23:25,750 --> 00:23:29,110
who are very very helpful they started challenging a few of my assumptions.

371
00:23:29,110 --> 00:23:31,110
on what I thought these design interviews were like.

372
00:23:31,110 --> 00:23:34,950
So as I was doing more mocks you know getting their sense of how I was doing.

373
00:23:34,950 --> 00:23:41,590
going from complete confusion on their faces to this growing clarity on how I was performing right. helped me kind of formulate my own way.

374
00:23:41,590 --> 00:23:43,430
And again, it's not a patent approach or anything.

375
00:23:43,430 --> 00:23:48,390
What helped me at the time was really breaking the problem down into a gathering all requirements,

376
00:23:48,390 --> 00:23:52,149
you know, functional requirements, non-functional requirements,

377
00:23:52,149 --> 00:23:53,750
and even extensions that you might want to consider.

378
00:23:53,750 --> 00:23:56,870
And then really going down and to talk about if you were to solve those requirements,

379
00:23:56,880 --> 00:23:59,909
how do you build the APIs or what would the APIs look like?

380
00:23:59,909 --> 00:24:04,230
What would be the entities and objects in your system you would store for serving those API those.

381
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:05,990
requirements?

382
00:24:05,990 --> 00:24:09,750
and then get to a point where you can draw on the board a very very high level.

383
00:24:09,750 --> 00:24:13,350
diagram of system that is purely just functional and not scalable.

384
00:24:13,350 --> 00:24:16,630
And if if that can take care of all your functional requirements in the first five.

385
00:24:16,630 --> 00:24:18,470
or 10 minutes, you are like a long way in.

386
00:24:18,470 --> 00:24:22,870
because that sets the stage for a showing that you thought about them on day one.

387
00:24:22,870 --> 00:24:27,350
and b cut down any ambiguity on where the interviewer might want to you know trick you with.

388
00:24:27,350 --> 00:24:30,630
And now is when you look at the non-functional requirements.

389
00:24:30,630 --> 00:24:32,549
Things like read should take no more than 10 millconds.

390
00:24:32,549 --> 00:24:34,630
Rise should not take more than 200 millconds.

391
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:37,190
Freshness SLOs's and so on.

392
00:24:37,190 --> 00:24:40,549
Now is the system that you have in place with one database and one server for example.

393
00:24:40,549 --> 00:24:44,789
Can it scale? Well, obviously can't. So how do we do this? Can you where can you scale?

394
00:24:44,789 --> 00:24:49,990
Where can you break the bottlenecks and systematically evolve grow each branch of.

395
00:24:49,990 --> 00:24:51,590
that design tree one piece at a time?

396
00:24:51,590 --> 00:24:57,909
So I think this structured thinking or this structured way kind of helped me break the problem down.

397
00:24:57,909 --> 00:25:01,110
And for me the back of the envelope math was something that I was usually good at.

398
00:25:01,110 --> 00:25:04,951
So that helped me kind of you know turn the problem to my like into space. that I was strong at.

399
00:25:04,951 --> 00:25:10,710
So if I can take a problem put the numbers on various bits of expectations on your system.

400
00:25:10,710 --> 00:25:13,590
then I can extend each one of those to see what solves it. and what trade-offs I got to make.

401
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:19,350
How do you go about communicating the tradeoffs effectively?

402
00:25:19,350 --> 00:25:23,990
So given my strings are actually being quantitative showing.

403
00:25:23,990 --> 00:25:25,990
that if I know a database for example can do a read in a millisecond.

404
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:30,231
it can do a write in 5 millconds for example. I can use that to show that.

405
00:25:30,231 --> 00:25:36,470
if I use this technology with these guarantees I would get these SLOs's that I'm meeting.

406
00:25:36,470 --> 00:25:39,190
but the cost of doing that might be literally high cost.

407
00:25:39,190 --> 00:25:42,549
for instance you might say that putting everything in memory.

408
00:25:42,549 --> 00:25:46,470
or in a very very highly performant database can get you set lows.

409
00:25:46,470 --> 00:25:49,669
but if your customers don't care or if your users don't care and if they're very cost sensitive.

410
00:25:49,669 --> 00:25:52,470
You might be okay with a low SLO in part system.

411
00:25:52,480 --> 00:25:55,750
So showing this with numbers is I think the easiest way to do it.

412
00:25:55,750 --> 00:25:57,430
It's also the most intuitive way as well.

413
00:25:57,430 --> 00:25:59,029
So numbers speak for themselves.

414
00:25:59,029 --> 00:26:02,149
You can show how one part can affect something else and what might be the rule for trade-off.

415
00:26:02,159 --> 00:26:06,310
Now I want to switch tracks considerably.

416
00:26:06,310 --> 00:26:10,310
We've been talking about the structure of the interviews and what the candidate faces.

417
00:26:10,310 --> 00:26:15,110
I want to look at it now from the interviewer's perspective.

418
00:26:15,110 --> 00:26:19,990
In a company, is the interviewer given instructions like do a system design interview.

419
00:26:19,990 --> 00:26:22,710
or do companies pretty much standardize.

420
00:26:22,710 --> 00:26:27,751
so everyone gets as close as possible to the same interview? The answer is yes to both.

421
00:26:27,760 --> 00:26:32,390
Typically, what happens when a company lacks a strong interviewer bench?

422
00:26:32,390 --> 00:26:36,710
They might be putting all their eggs on a single like one. or two very senior engineers in the company.

423
00:26:36,710 --> 00:26:41,190
If you think of a startup, that person is usually a CTO.

424
00:26:41,190 --> 00:26:43,590
and that CTO is most likely going to be using their set of problems.

425
00:26:43,590 --> 00:26:47,190
that they have or they might have in the next 3 to 6 months.

426
00:26:47,190 --> 00:26:48,710
and use that as a basis for throwing problems at the candidate.

427
00:26:48,710 --> 00:26:51,750
which is not a bad thing because you're testing for practical applications.

428
00:26:51,750 --> 00:26:59,110
As companies grow, what happens is there is a bigger emphasis on being fair, not being biased,

429
00:26:59,110 --> 00:27:04,310
and ensuring that all candidates have a, you know, fairly similar interview bar and experience.

430
00:27:04,320 --> 00:27:10,149
without it being a thing that you can look off Reddit and learn the answer for.

431
00:27:10,149 --> 00:27:14,950
So, companies actually invest in interviewer training so that they can remove some of the bias language, they can be more focused,

432
00:27:14,950 --> 00:27:18,149
they can actually be more targeted towards the candidates's experience.

433
00:27:18,149 --> 00:27:23,909
You don't want a front-end engineer asking a very fairly heavily front- end focused question for a candidate.

434
00:27:23,909 --> 00:27:27,511
who's probably a backend engineer or a platform engineer or in engineer. and vice versa.

435
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:33,669
So, companies invest in both training and domain based interviewing skills and interviewing coaching, right?

436
00:27:33,669 --> 00:27:35,510
They also do this for leveling as well, right?

437
00:27:35,520 --> 00:27:38,549
They want to make sure that everybody at a certain level has similar bar.

438
00:27:38,549 --> 00:27:41,350
and being is being assessed in a similar way.

439
00:27:41,350 --> 00:27:43,269
So, they can remove some of the bias around how these interviews happen.

440
00:27:43,279 --> 00:27:51,029
