that would help me with this kind of SLO. Any vendor you want that's fine.

658
00:41:38,000 --> 00:41:41,430
In fact, I would only use estim at the end.

659
00:41:41,430 --> 00:41:46,069
where I would go look because I need a thousand hosts today's EC2 prices, EC2 instance prices are.

660
00:41:46,069 --> 00:41:48,309
I don't know I think it's $7 for a mini or whatnot, right?

661
00:41:48,319 --> 00:41:50,230
This is what it's going to cost you.

662
00:41:50,230 --> 00:41:52,390
So based on that cost, this is your capex.

663
00:41:52,400 --> 00:41:53,990
What can you do?

664
00:41:53,990 --> 00:41:55,750
And I would say one other misconception is.

665
00:41:55,750 --> 00:42:01,190
that people think a system design interview is purely purely technical.

666
00:42:01,190 --> 00:42:06,309
It is by and large a communication and time and expectations management interview.

667
00:42:06,309 --> 00:42:09,349
I think we've covered that a few times,

668
00:42:09,349 --> 00:42:15,510
but was there anything way you wanted to expand or elaborate on that last point that we haven't?

669
00:42:15,520 --> 00:42:19,030
Yeah. So, if I was to add something new to it, I mean, think about this.

670
00:42:19,030 --> 00:42:27,910
As I said before, there are 30 problems in the system design interview problem set, right?

671
00:42:27,910 --> 00:42:30,150
Like 30 or so problems like there's Uber,

672
00:42:30,150 --> 00:42:32,950
Bitly, Dropbox, Ticket Master, Tinder, like there's a few standard problems, right?

673
00:42:32,950 --> 00:42:34,550
And still people struggle with it.

674
00:42:34,550 --> 00:42:37,750
I mean how hard double quotes how hard can it be to master 30 problems.

675
00:42:37,750 --> 00:42:39,430
but if you think about it people fail or people struggle.

676
00:42:39,440 --> 00:42:43,670
because they look at it as I need to go there draw a bunch of boxes.

677
00:42:43,670 --> 00:42:46,309
and it's all an aha moment from there.

678
00:42:46,309 --> 00:42:48,230
but imagine the last product that was built.

679
00:42:48,230 --> 00:42:51,430
it was not built because somebody decided to throw in boxes.

680
00:42:51,430 --> 00:42:53,270
it was built because there was a user need that they had to understand.

681
00:42:53,280 --> 00:42:56,710
there was a business requirement or business need that they had to solve.

682
00:42:56,710 --> 00:42:59,910
and they had constraints they didn't have oodles of funding on day one.

683
00:42:59,910 --> 00:43:03,670
they had to go with three boxes and then incrementally make improvements.

684
00:43:03,670 --> 00:43:07,190
even if they were bad ones because they were the right ones at that point in time.

685
00:43:07,190 --> 00:43:09,910
like that doesn't happen in isolation it happens through communication.

686
00:43:09,910 --> 00:43:15,349
it happens through leadership it happens through explaining and articulating what you're doing why you're doing.

687
00:43:15,349 --> 00:43:17,750
and you know when you can come back to it.

688
00:43:17,750 --> 00:43:21,589
so as a senior engineer a lot of your time goes into articulating all these.

689
00:43:21,589 --> 00:43:23,829
rather than just writing code or building systems.

690
00:43:23,829 --> 00:43:25,589
and I think being good at that articulation.

691
00:43:25,599 --> 00:43:30,790
and the non-technical aspect helps you do well in these interviews too.

692
00:43:30,800 --> 00:43:36,390
Sam I think that's a great place to wrap up to close out the show.

693
